 "content": "# Noisy motif translations\n\nUse this branch when a noisy grid has one distinctive single-color motif amid zeros and another background color. Record the motif's colored-cell offsets from its bounding box. Search for translated copies whose motif positions all contain zeros, and recolor those matching positions with the motif color. Preserve every other cell, including the original motif and zeros/background at non-motif positions inside the bounding box. Do not assume flood-fill or neighbor propagation: test motif translations when the grid looks like noisy background plus one seed object. Account for the seed shape and legal translations within bounds; allow rotation or reflection only when demonstrations support it.\n"
}

{
 "path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-openclaw-luna6-high-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u012/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/continual-arc/patterns/separator-tile-classification.md",
 "content": "# Separator tile classification\n\nUse this branch when full-width and full-height separator lines partition the grid into equal tiles and the output dimensions match that tile layout. Map each tile to one output cell. Use demonstrations to determine which in-tile feature or count controls the output; do not assume every unusual cell or single marker is positive. For the observed task pattern with color-8 separators, 2×3 tiles containing mostly color 3 and occasional color 2, emit 1 for a tile with at least two 2s and 3 otherwise. Treat that threshold and color mapping as specific to that demonstrated pattern, not as a general tile rule.\n"
}

{
 "path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-openclaw-luna6-high-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u012/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/continual-arc/patterns/edge-continuation.md",
 "content": "# Edge continuation\n\nUse this branch when demonstrations show color 1 marking an absent continuation at a grid edge. Compare repeated rows, bands, or motif copies to infer the expected continuation and affected cells. It may be a solid suffix or only selected motif-phase positions. Color only confirmed missing continuation cells; preserve the existing motif and unrelated cells. Check every demo's changed and unchanged cells, and choose repeated copies from that evidence—not from rightmost zeros or suffix length alone.\n"
}
